WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.451
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.092 --> 00:00:07.800
CARTER MORGAN: Welcome
to another episode

00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:10.980
of "VM End to End," a show
where we have a VM skeptic

00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:12.960
and a VM enthusiast
come together,

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:17.640
hash out all things cloud,
computer, compute-related.

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:19.800
Brian, thank you so much
for being here today.

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:21.133
I've got some questions for you.

00:00:21.133 --> 00:00:22.258
BRIAN DORSEY: Hello, hello.

00:00:22.258 --> 00:00:23.250
I'm excited today.

00:00:23.250 --> 00:00:26.230
Databases and VMs,
it's my happy place.

00:00:26.230 --> 00:00:28.200
And because of that,
I wanted to bring

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:30.300
in Gabe Weiss, who's
one of our colleagues

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:31.630
and expert in databases.

00:00:31.630 --> 00:00:32.130
Hi, Gabe.

00:00:32.130 --> 00:00:33.255
GABE WEISS: Hey, everybody.

00:00:33.255 --> 00:00:34.980
CARTER MORGAN: Yes,
happy to get into it.

00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:37.800
I guess, I'm just going to
dive right in, if that's cool.

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:42.120
I want to know, when it
comes to data on the Cloud

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:44.550
with compute and
whatnot, why not

00:00:44.550 --> 00:00:47.100
just let a managed service
run it for me magically?

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:49.110
Why would I want
to run the database

00:00:49.110 --> 00:00:50.970
level at the compute level?

00:00:50.970 --> 00:00:52.080
Why does that make sense?

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:54.330
GABE WEISS: I mean,
why not, indeed, right?

00:00:54.330 --> 00:00:58.000
The dev answer that we
always give, it depends.

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:00.660
There is nothing wrong
with running a database

00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:02.290
on a VM in the Cloud.

00:01:02.290 --> 00:01:04.800
Let's be clear right away.

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:08.010
Cloud SQL, which is a relational
database managed offering,

00:01:08.010 --> 00:01:09.210
it runs in a VM.

00:01:09.210 --> 00:01:11.302
So whether you're running
in your VM or our VM,

00:01:11.302 --> 00:01:12.510
you're still running in a VM.

00:01:12.510 --> 00:01:16.230
The difference is, and why
you might want to run your own

00:01:16.230 --> 00:01:18.370
really comes down to
one of requirements.

00:01:18.370 --> 00:01:21.442
If you have a legacy
system, you've

00:01:21.442 --> 00:01:23.400
got an application you've
been running forever,

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:26.040
and it has a set
of requirements,

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:28.780
then a managed database
might not satisfy those.

00:01:28.780 --> 00:01:30.540
One of the things
with managed databases

00:01:30.540 --> 00:01:33.360
is we manage it for you,
which means we restrict

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:35.040
what you can do with it, right?

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:36.930
BRIAN DORSEY: OK, so
let's be specific.

00:01:36.930 --> 00:01:39.420
What are the kinds
of things that

00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:41.610
might push you into running
it yourself on a VM?

00:01:41.610 --> 00:01:42.360
GABE WEISS: Right.

00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:44.350
So I bring up-- like, if
you've got this legacy

00:01:44.350 --> 00:01:46.350
system, if you've got an
application that you've

00:01:46.350 --> 00:01:48.523
been running for a
couple of decades,

00:01:48.523 --> 00:01:50.940
it builds up over time-- what
you add to it, when you have

00:01:50.940 --> 00:01:52.260
requirements, what you need.

00:01:52.260 --> 00:01:54.570
So a real good concrete
example of this

00:01:54.570 --> 00:01:58.050
is, take SQL Server, one of
the big things with SQL servers

00:01:58.050 --> 00:01:59.340
are stored procedures.

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:02.310
You've got these methods that
run on the database itself.

00:02:02.310 --> 00:02:04.770
Stored procedures have certain
requirements sometimes.

00:02:04.770 --> 00:02:07.530
Like, maybe they
require a super user.

00:02:07.530 --> 00:02:09.580
If you're running on
a managed database,

00:02:09.580 --> 00:02:11.310
we don't give you
a proper super user

00:02:11.310 --> 00:02:14.700
because we have to protect
the database for you.

00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:16.590
We have an SLA, a
Service Level Agreement,

00:02:16.590 --> 00:02:18.990
where we guarantee that
the database is going

00:02:18.990 --> 00:02:21.690
to be up all the time for you.

00:02:21.690 --> 00:02:23.610
You hear some people
talk about the nines.

00:02:23.610 --> 00:02:27.870
The nines is your percent of
how much uptime we guarantee.

00:02:27.870 --> 00:02:29.850
Cloud SQL, for example,
our managed offering,

00:02:29.850 --> 00:02:32.760
is 99.95% available--

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:35.040
3 and 1/2 nines,
which means it's

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:37.660
like 20 minutes of downtime a
month or something like that.

00:02:37.660 --> 00:02:42.510
So with these extensions,
or the stored procedures,

00:02:42.510 --> 00:02:45.240
we have to guard that
uptime, which means

00:02:45.240 --> 00:02:46.780
we don't give you everything.

00:02:46.780 --> 00:02:49.470
So if you've got a system
that requires that,

00:02:49.470 --> 00:02:51.450
a managed system might
not work for you.

00:02:51.450 --> 00:02:52.200
CARTER MORGAN: OK.

00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:54.760
That actually makes
a lot of sense.

00:02:54.760 --> 00:02:57.760
And I appreciate you for
bringing in that perspective.

00:02:57.760 --> 00:03:00.180
So now, my question
is, what about someone

00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:04.380
that maybe doesn't have
their database in the Cloud

00:03:04.380 --> 00:03:06.390
yet but they're thinking
about switching over?

00:03:06.390 --> 00:03:07.740
What's the process for that?

00:03:07.740 --> 00:03:08.490
GABE WEISS: Right.

00:03:08.490 --> 00:03:12.000
So you're talking about-- we
call it the green field, where

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:15.143
new application, a new
company that's thinking,

00:03:15.143 --> 00:03:16.560
like, where should
I put my stuff?

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:17.935
Everything's moving
to the Cloud.

00:03:17.935 --> 00:03:18.750
Let's be clear.

00:03:18.750 --> 00:03:20.822
This what we're seeing
in the industry,

00:03:20.822 --> 00:03:22.530
is everyone wants
their data in the Cloud

00:03:22.530 --> 00:03:23.730
for a whole host of reasons.

00:03:23.730 --> 00:03:26.220
You want to run
analytics on your data.

00:03:26.220 --> 00:03:28.380
You've got a global
situation where

00:03:28.380 --> 00:03:30.540
you've got an application
that needs global access.

00:03:30.540 --> 00:03:31.873
You're going to be in the Cloud.

00:03:31.873 --> 00:03:35.640
So I always say, if you
haven't got something already,

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:37.242
start with the managed service.

00:03:37.242 --> 00:03:39.450
You don't want to set up
all of the boilerplate stuff

00:03:39.450 --> 00:03:40.500
that you're going
to have to do to run

00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:43.560
your own database-- the hardware
management, all the operating

00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:46.320
system and security updates,
managing the database health

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:49.170
itself, making sure that
the machine is healthy.

00:03:49.170 --> 00:03:51.420
With a managed service, all
of that's handled for you.

00:03:51.420 --> 00:03:53.260
You don't have to do
any of that stuff.

00:03:53.260 --> 00:03:55.320
So if you're starting
fresh, absolutely,

00:03:55.320 --> 00:03:57.000
start with a managed service.

00:03:57.000 --> 00:03:59.220
BRIAN DORSEY: Well, you
just set it right there.

00:03:59.220 --> 00:04:00.690
I love it when you're
starting a new project,

00:04:00.690 --> 00:04:02.610
and you get to set
everything up from scratch.

00:04:02.610 --> 00:04:06.000
But a lot of us are dealing
with a lot of software

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:07.170
that already exists.

00:04:07.170 --> 00:04:08.545
What do you do in that scenario?

00:04:08.545 --> 00:04:10.920
GABE WEISS: So if you've got
one of those legacy systems,

00:04:10.920 --> 00:04:13.230
like I said, like the
20-year application you're

00:04:13.230 --> 00:04:16.896
working with, there's a few
different ways you could do it.

00:04:16.896 --> 00:04:19.320
If your systems are all
really tightly integrated,

00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:22.170
and you've got it running and
a tight cluster of machines

00:04:22.170 --> 00:04:25.320
or something, you can just
do that, the lift and shift.

00:04:25.320 --> 00:04:27.788
Just take the entire
thing, throw it up in a VM.

00:04:27.788 --> 00:04:29.580
And now, instead of
running in your closet,

00:04:29.580 --> 00:04:30.788
you're running in our closet.

00:04:30.788 --> 00:04:32.970
But we handle all of the
machine health for you.

00:04:32.970 --> 00:04:35.220
So that's absolutely
a valid strategy.

00:04:35.220 --> 00:04:37.890
If you need to get out of where
you're at and get to the Cloud

00:04:37.890 --> 00:04:40.650
first, just put everything
in a VM and get going.

00:04:40.650 --> 00:04:42.810
You do want to split the
data out, though, right?

00:04:42.810 --> 00:04:46.470
So strategically speaking,
if your requirements get

00:04:46.470 --> 00:04:47.640
to the Cloud first, do that.

00:04:47.640 --> 00:04:49.182
Lift and shift up,
then start working

00:04:49.182 --> 00:04:50.920
on splitting of your data.

00:04:50.920 --> 00:04:53.800
There are cases, though, where
that might not be the case.

00:04:53.800 --> 00:04:58.140
If you've got an application
that, for compliance

00:04:58.140 --> 00:05:01.260
reasons or other-- you have
to have the application local,

00:05:01.260 --> 00:05:04.110
but your data has now grown
beyond where it can handle,

00:05:04.110 --> 00:05:05.940
and you need to just
get the data out,

00:05:05.940 --> 00:05:09.055
splitting the data out
is totally fine too.

00:05:09.055 --> 00:05:10.680
But there's some
problems there, right?

00:05:10.680 --> 00:05:14.130
So the internet-- the
internet's a problem, right?

00:05:14.130 --> 00:05:16.445
So if you are in
a situation where

00:05:16.445 --> 00:05:17.820
you want to keep
your application

00:05:17.820 --> 00:05:19.770
but you need to move
the data, that's fine.

00:05:19.770 --> 00:05:21.300
But you should be
aware that you'll

00:05:21.300 --> 00:05:23.860
have to handle that on
the application side.

00:05:23.860 --> 00:05:27.200
So great connection
to your data center.

00:05:27.200 --> 00:05:29.450
Maybe you've got a
30-millisecond ping time round

00:05:29.450 --> 00:05:30.410
trip.

00:05:30.410 --> 00:05:32.900
If you're running locally,
your ping to your database

00:05:32.900 --> 00:05:35.120
is less than a millisecond
for a really well-tuned

00:05:35.120 --> 00:05:36.330
relational database.

00:05:36.330 --> 00:05:37.940
So all of a sudden,
your time is 30.

00:05:37.940 --> 00:05:41.210
But it's not even that
kind because, let's say,

00:05:41.210 --> 00:05:44.270
for example, you're only
allowing SSL connections

00:05:44.270 --> 00:05:45.230
to your database.

00:05:45.230 --> 00:05:47.960
The TLS handshake to
establish that connection

00:05:47.960 --> 00:05:49.730
takes two round
trips back and forth

00:05:49.730 --> 00:05:52.560
to establish the handshake
before you get to the data.

00:05:52.560 --> 00:05:55.100
So now, it's not 30
milliseconds to get your answer.

00:05:55.100 --> 00:05:56.817
It's 100 milliseconds
to get your answer.

00:05:56.817 --> 00:05:58.400
BRIAN DORSEY: And
then a bunch of apps

00:05:58.400 --> 00:06:01.280
make a bunch of queries in
a row, and so that just--

00:06:01.280 --> 00:06:03.170
every one of those
extends it out.

00:06:03.170 --> 00:06:05.870
So what should people do
in that kind of-- what

00:06:05.870 --> 00:06:06.890
do you do about that?

00:06:06.890 --> 00:06:08.360
GABE WEISS: I mean, you
can't fix the internet.

00:06:08.360 --> 00:06:10.027
The internet, you're
kind of stuck with.

00:06:10.027 --> 00:06:13.663
But the tons of connections, the
queries that you talked about,

00:06:13.663 --> 00:06:15.080
some applications,
because they're

00:06:15.080 --> 00:06:16.850
used to this quick
turnaround time,

00:06:16.850 --> 00:06:19.250
they'll just spin up a
connection, ask their question,

00:06:19.250 --> 00:06:20.330
shut down the connection.

00:06:20.330 --> 00:06:22.820
If you do that a couple hundred
times, and your round trip

00:06:22.820 --> 00:06:25.280
is now not so
quick, that's going

00:06:25.280 --> 00:06:28.220
to wreck your responsive UI.

00:06:28.220 --> 00:06:30.263
So instead, the
first thing that I

00:06:30.263 --> 00:06:32.180
tell people is, the first
thing you want to do

00:06:32.180 --> 00:06:34.700
is what we call client-side
connection pooling.

00:06:34.700 --> 00:06:37.940
Most libraries that you
connect with-- so for Python,

00:06:37.940 --> 00:06:41.000
we recommend SQL Alchemy
is a great library

00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:43.190
to use for connecting to
your relational databases.

00:06:43.190 --> 00:06:46.940
There's objects that you use
to create the connection,

00:06:46.940 --> 00:06:49.640
and there's a connection object,
and there's a pool object.

00:06:49.640 --> 00:06:52.760
That pool is a
connection pool that

00:06:52.760 --> 00:06:55.380
will establish-- it hands you
a connection to the database,

00:06:55.380 --> 00:06:58.382
and it holds it open for
whatever you specify.

00:06:58.382 --> 00:07:00.590
That means if you're making
these hundreds of queries

00:07:00.590 --> 00:07:02.190
every second, that's great.

00:07:02.190 --> 00:07:04.460
It'll establish one connection,
run all your queries

00:07:04.460 --> 00:07:06.800
until there's some idle
time, then shut it down.

00:07:06.800 --> 00:07:08.525
So that cuts down
your round-trip time

00:07:08.525 --> 00:07:10.400
because the round-trip
time for that security

00:07:10.400 --> 00:07:12.290
is only on that
initial connection.

00:07:12.290 --> 00:07:14.115
And then after that,
you're good to go.

00:07:14.115 --> 00:07:14.990
CARTER MORGAN: Right.

00:07:14.990 --> 00:07:18.502
This is really interesting
to me because I

00:07:18.502 --> 00:07:20.960
like that you said earlier, if
you're starting green field,

00:07:20.960 --> 00:07:21.680
go managed.

00:07:21.680 --> 00:07:22.880
Use the managed service.

00:07:22.880 --> 00:07:24.725
That's the easy move.

00:07:24.725 --> 00:07:27.350
And then I can see how it gets
a lot more complicated depending

00:07:27.350 --> 00:07:28.585
on your needs.

00:07:28.585 --> 00:07:30.710
The one thing I think that
shouldn't be complicated

00:07:30.710 --> 00:07:32.690
is actually moving to the Cloud.

00:07:32.690 --> 00:07:35.870
And so are there
tools or anything

00:07:35.870 --> 00:07:38.450
to help make that
a smoother process?

00:07:38.450 --> 00:07:40.580
GABE WEISS: Like you
said, it shouldn't be.

00:07:40.580 --> 00:07:42.890
And there are absolutely
cases where it is, right?

00:07:42.890 --> 00:07:45.950
There's totally cases where
just moving the data is hard.

00:07:45.950 --> 00:07:48.200
One of the biggest
problems with moving

00:07:48.200 --> 00:07:50.330
your data is-- the
boilerplate way

00:07:50.330 --> 00:07:52.670
to do this in a lot
of cases is what

00:07:52.670 --> 00:07:55.490
they call the export/import,
which is go to your database,

00:07:55.490 --> 00:07:57.560
you hit a button, and it
dumps all of your data

00:07:57.560 --> 00:07:58.700
to this giant file.

00:07:58.700 --> 00:08:00.740
And then on the new
instance, you hit Import,

00:08:00.740 --> 00:08:03.240
and it sucks it all into the
new one, and you're good to go.

00:08:03.240 --> 00:08:06.510
That's the old-school classic
way that you do this stuff.

00:08:06.510 --> 00:08:08.720
But if you've got
terabytes of data,

00:08:08.720 --> 00:08:10.470
it's kind of tough to do that.

00:08:10.470 --> 00:08:13.100
And while you're doing the
import, well, now what?

00:08:13.100 --> 00:08:15.308
Unless you shut everyone
down and say, OK, no on

00:08:15.308 --> 00:08:16.100
touch the database.

00:08:16.100 --> 00:08:17.300
We're doing a migration.

00:08:17.300 --> 00:08:19.400
Now, you've got data
that continues to come in

00:08:19.400 --> 00:08:20.420
while you're importing.

00:08:20.420 --> 00:08:23.492
So the gap between when
you've hit the export button

00:08:23.492 --> 00:08:24.950
and when you do
the import, there's

00:08:24.950 --> 00:08:26.533
this data that you're
missing that you

00:08:26.533 --> 00:08:27.800
have to catch up still.

00:08:27.800 --> 00:08:29.175
There's other ways
you can do it.

00:08:29.175 --> 00:08:33.169
You can set up replication
between two servers, which

00:08:33.169 --> 00:08:35.450
basically means the current
database that you're

00:08:35.450 --> 00:08:37.317
moving away from, you
set up as the primary.

00:08:37.317 --> 00:08:38.900
You set up a replica,
and you say, OK,

00:08:38.900 --> 00:08:40.039
now move all the data over.

00:08:40.039 --> 00:08:40.706
And it'll do it.

00:08:40.706 --> 00:08:42.980
It streams it across the pipe.

00:08:42.980 --> 00:08:44.240
It's kind of a pain to set up.

00:08:44.240 --> 00:08:45.890
There's a bunch of little knobs.

00:08:45.890 --> 00:08:47.810
To make that easier,
we've got a service

00:08:47.810 --> 00:08:51.140
it's called Database
Migration Service, DMS.

00:08:51.140 --> 00:08:52.400
And that basically handles it.

00:08:52.400 --> 00:08:54.810
It still uses the database
native ways to do it.

00:08:54.810 --> 00:08:57.440
So, for example, on
Postgres, we use PG Logical

00:08:57.440 --> 00:08:59.180
as the way we do migrations.

00:08:59.180 --> 00:09:02.522
And it will handle all
of the connections, all

00:09:02.522 --> 00:09:03.230
the connectivity.

00:09:03.230 --> 00:09:07.280
You define a source object,
you source database in the UI.

00:09:07.280 --> 00:09:09.920
And then it creates a
Cloud SQL instance for you.

00:09:09.920 --> 00:09:10.940
And then it moves it.

00:09:10.940 --> 00:09:13.582
Now, keeping in mind, this
is only for managed service.

00:09:13.582 --> 00:09:15.290
So if you're moving
to a managed service,

00:09:15.290 --> 00:09:16.430
this is the tool to do it.

00:09:16.430 --> 00:09:19.885
If you want to run your own in
a VM, you're kind of on your own

00:09:19.885 --> 00:09:21.260
because it's not
managed for you.

00:09:21.260 --> 00:09:22.850
So you have to do
all of these pieces.

00:09:22.850 --> 00:09:23.683
You can still do it.

00:09:23.683 --> 00:09:26.270
There's still ways to
set up external replicas

00:09:26.270 --> 00:09:28.940
and external primary
instances to do it.

00:09:28.940 --> 00:09:29.910
But you're on your own.

00:09:29.910 --> 00:09:32.900
So DMS is the easy way to
move to a managed service.

00:09:32.900 --> 00:09:35.442
BRIAN DORSEY: OK, that's super
cool because I have definitely

00:09:35.442 --> 00:09:38.360
lived the try to schedule
a window where you can shut

00:09:38.360 --> 00:09:40.598
everything down with
all the people involved,

00:09:40.598 --> 00:09:42.140
and then hope
everything works right,

00:09:42.140 --> 00:09:44.450
and you don't have to revert
or whatever everything.

00:09:44.450 --> 00:09:46.310
And that's hard.

00:09:46.310 --> 00:09:48.830
So it's nice to know
that there's some tools

00:09:48.830 --> 00:09:49.910
to make that easier now.

00:09:49.910 --> 00:09:52.460
CARTER MORGAN: So to summarize
what we just learned today,

00:09:52.460 --> 00:09:55.670
just to recap everything
you came and taught,

00:09:55.670 --> 00:09:59.570
I still want to hold up
that managed databases can

00:09:59.570 --> 00:10:01.845
and should be used in
a lot of instances.

00:10:01.845 --> 00:10:04.220
But there are other instances
where it doesn't make sense

00:10:04.220 --> 00:10:06.590
to use them, where there's
some capability that you need

00:10:06.590 --> 00:10:09.950
that's not provided
yet, or maybe just

00:10:09.950 --> 00:10:11.840
the way your system is set up.

00:10:11.840 --> 00:10:13.970
But if you're trying
to transfer over,

00:10:13.970 --> 00:10:16.190
then there's Database
Management System,

00:10:16.190 --> 00:10:18.830
which makes it a lot
smoother of a process.

00:10:18.830 --> 00:10:19.550
Did I get that?

00:10:19.550 --> 00:10:20.240
GABE WEISS: Exactly.

00:10:20.240 --> 00:10:22.323
And it all comes down to
your requirements, right?

00:10:22.323 --> 00:10:24.670
Like you said, nothing wrong
with running a database

00:10:24.670 --> 00:10:26.837
a VM as long as you're
comfortable managing it.

00:10:26.837 --> 00:10:28.420
If you don't want
to worry about that,

00:10:28.420 --> 00:10:29.750
managed services are great.

00:10:29.750 --> 00:10:32.208
BRIAN DORSEY: So if people want
to dig in and kind of learn

00:10:32.208 --> 00:10:34.690
more about this, do you
have any recommendations?

00:10:34.690 --> 00:10:35.565
GABE WEISS: Oh, yeah.

00:10:35.565 --> 00:10:39.207
So the DMS, the Database
Migration Service documentation

00:10:39.207 --> 00:10:40.790
on our site is a
great place to start.

00:10:40.790 --> 00:10:42.370
There's a lot of
good links there.

00:10:42.370 --> 00:10:45.610
It talks a lot about the
strategies that I talked about,

00:10:45.610 --> 00:10:47.920
and it will lead
you into getting

00:10:47.920 --> 00:10:50.260
comfortable with the idea
of moving your data around.

00:10:50.260 --> 00:10:52.135
For other stuff, there's
a ton of blog posts.

00:10:52.135 --> 00:10:55.660
We've got a lot of blog
material on our GCP blog

00:10:55.660 --> 00:11:00.100
around v managed service
versus running your own.

00:11:00.100 --> 00:11:01.150
There's troubleshooting.

00:11:01.150 --> 00:11:02.740
There's a couple of ones that
are like troubleshooting.

00:11:02.740 --> 00:11:04.090
I wrote one on connectivity.

00:11:04.090 --> 00:11:06.770
Networking is really
hard just in general.

00:11:06.770 --> 00:11:08.710
So I have a good
post on networking

00:11:08.710 --> 00:11:10.188
between different services.

00:11:10.188 --> 00:11:11.980
CARTER MORGAN: Well,
yo, thank you so much.

00:11:11.980 --> 00:11:13.360
Thank you, Gabe, for being here.

00:11:13.360 --> 00:11:15.500
Brian, as always, thank
you for being here.

00:11:15.500 --> 00:11:17.530
And if you're watching
at home, leave a comment.

00:11:17.530 --> 00:11:19.570
Let us know anything new that
you've learned or picked up.

00:11:19.570 --> 00:11:21.290
We definitely
check the comments.

00:11:21.290 --> 00:11:22.315
Thank you so much.

00:11:22.315 --> 00:11:23.440
That's the end of this one.

00:11:23.440 --> 00:11:27.090
[MUSIC PLAYING]

